#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# to_number_field

#% text
# `to_number_field(a, theta)` gives `a` as `algebraic_number(theta, [c0, c1, ..., c(d-1)])` with `a = c0 + c1 theta + ... + c(d-1) theta^(d-1)`, where `d` is the degree of the algebraic number `theta` (a rational stays a rational; the coefficient list is the last part of the result).

#% text
# `to_number_field(a)` of one number is `to_number_field(a, a)`: `a` generates its own field, `algebraic_number(a, [0, 1, 0, ...])`.

#% text
# `to_number_field([a, b, ...])` (also with `all` as second argument) gives the list of the numbers as algebraic numbers of one field generated by a primitive element `gamma` of the field of all of them.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The numbers may be built like those of `minimal_polynomial`: rationals, complex rationals, radicals and root objects with sums, products and powers.

#% item
# When `a` does not lie in the field of `theta`, the result is `failure(not_in_field, to_number_field, ...)`.

#% item
# The primitive element is `b + s a` for the first integer `s` in 0, 1, -1, 2, ... at which a norm is square-free (Trager's method).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# sqrt(2) in the field of sqrt(2) + sqrt(3):

#% cell
to_number_field(sqrt(2), sqrt(2) + sqrt(3))
#% output
#= algebraic_number(root(&(1 - 10*&1^2 + &1^4), 4), [0, -9/2, 0, 1/2])

#% text
# One number as the generator of its field:

#% cell
to_number_field(root(&(&1^5 + &1 + 11), 1))
#% output
#= algebraic_number(root(&(11 + &1 + &1^5), 1), [0, 1, 0, 0, 0])

#% text
# One field for sqrt(2) and sqrt(3):

#% cell
to_number_field([sqrt(2), sqrt(3)])
#% output
#= [algebraic_number(root(&(1 - 10*&1^2 + &1^4), 4), [0, -9/2, 0, 1/2]), algebraic_number(root(&(1 - 10*&1^2 + &1^4), 4), [0, 11/2, 0, -1/2])]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# sqrt(2) lies in the field of 2^(1/4):

#% cell
to_number_field(sqrt(2), 2^(1/4))
#% output
#= algebraic_number(root(&(-2 + &1^4), 2), [0, 0, 1, 0])

#% text
# sqrt(3) does not lie in the field of sqrt(2):

#% cell
to_number_field(sqrt(3), sqrt(2))
#% output
#! failure(not_in_field, to_number_field, "the number does not lie in the field of the generator")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Check a representation by substitution:

#% cell
expand((-9/2)*(sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)) + (1/2)*(sqrt(2) + sqrt(3))^3)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)

